course: dictionary
lesson: 12844
title: Encroachment: creeping into someone else's space
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Encroachment means slowly going into a place, time or right that belongs to someone else, a little at a time. «Each winter the sea makes a steady encroachment on the beach.» The sea does not jump onto the sand all at once. It creeps forward bit by bit, taking more land each year.
explain: Look for the clue words. Slowly, a little at a time, and spreading are all clues. «The desert's encroachment forced the village to move.» The sand spreads over the land, so the village has less space every year. Encroachment is a slow taking over of space.
explain: The word can also be about land or rights that belong to a person. «Building a fence onto your neighbour's garden is an encroachment on their land.» Notice the word «on» after encroachment. To check your meaning, swap in «creeping into» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Encroachment is a noun, so you can have one encroachment or several encroachments.
fact: In law, encroachment means building or using land beyond your boundary, onto someone else's land.
fact: In nature, encroachment can mean the sea, a desert or towns slowly spreading over land.
fact: Useful phrases are «encroachment on» and «the encroachment of the sea».
fact: In American football, encroachment is a foul called when a player moves into the other team's space too early.
exercise: «The sea's encroachment swallowed up a little more beach every winter.» What does encroachment mean here? | A sudden storm that clears the beach | A kind of seashell | *Slow creeping over land that is not its own | A sandcastle competition
exercise: «Building the new shed onto Mrs Patel's garden was an encroachment on her land.» What does this mean? | Giving her a gift of land | *Building beyond the boundary, onto land that is not yours | Planting flowers in a park | Measuring her garden for a map
exercise: The referee shouts «Encroachment!» in an American football game. What has happened? | The crowd has cheered too loudly | A player has scored six points | The ball has gone out of bounds | *A player has moved into space that is not his before the ball is snapped
test: «The desert's encroachment forced the village to move.» What is the best meaning of encroachment? | A quick trip to the desert | Sand being dug up for a sandcastle | *Sand slowly spreading over the land | A sweet, cool breeze blowing over the village
test: Lia says «encroachment on my rights» means someone is slowly taking what belongs to her. Is Lia right? | No, it means she has lost all her rights | No, it means her rights are very small | No, it means someone is giving her a present | *Yes, it means someone is slowly taking what belongs to her
exam: «Each year the town's encroachment on the forest meant fewer trees and less space for the deer.» What does encroachment mean in this sentence? | A large picnic in the park | Deer running fast through the woods | *Towns slowly spreading into the forest | Trees growing taller each spring
